 "result": "All of this is in `src/tessel/views.py`, plus one hook in `src/tessel/sansio/app.py`.\n\n1. **`VerbView.__init_subclass__`** (`views.py:165`): when you subclass, it scans for the attributes in `http_method_funcs` (`get`, `post`, …). It sets `cls.methods` to the uppercased set, merged with any base class's `methods`. This is how defining `get()` and `post()` yields `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`.\n\n2. **`View.to_view_func(name, ...)`** (`views.py:86`): this classmethod builds a plain `view(**kwargs)` function.\n   - With `init_every_request=True`, it instantiates the class on every request. Otherwise it instantiates once.\n   - Either way it calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)` through `active_app.ensure_sync`.\n   - It applies `cls.decorators`, then copies `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` onto the function (`:129-134`).\n\n3. **`VerbView.run_view`** (`views.py:182`): this is the per-method routing. It does `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`, falls back to `get` for HEAD when there is no `head` handler, and calls the result with the URL kwargs.\n\n4. **Registration** (`App.attach_route_rule` in `src/tessel/sansio/app.py:~611-652`): it reads `view_func.methods` and `view_func.provide_automatic_options`, so the URL rule is created with the right allowed methods.",
